<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Lippe</h1><p class="st-meta">1789 CE – 1918 CE · capital Detmold</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Westphalian principality at Detmold that survived as a German state into the 20th century and played a part in Hitler's rise.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The lords of Lippe held lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemgo">Lemgo</a> from the 12th century, becoming counts in 1528. Though granted princely rank in 1720, they did not use the title until Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-prince-of-lippe">Leopold I</a> accepted it in 1789, when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-lippe">County of Lippe</a>-Detmold became the Principality of Lippe.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Princess Pauline, regent from 1802 to 1820, kept Lippe independent by joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and introduced social reforms, including a nursery school. Lippe was rural and Calvinist, and many men worked seasonally as brickmakers elsewhere. A disputed succession between 1895 and 1905, involving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> William II's brother-in-law, was settled by arbitration in favour of the Biesterfeld line.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lippe joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iv-prince-of-lippe">Prince Leopold IV</a> abdicated in November 1918, and the state continued as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-lippe">Free State of Lippe</a> until 1947.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-lippe.jpg" alt="Principality of Lippe" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Fornax · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%BCrstentum_Lippe_c_1858-_c_1880.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1789 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Detmold</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Leopold I, Pauline, Leopold IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Lippe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=principality-of-lippe" aria-label="Principality of Lippe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4880 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Lippe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5200 638L5200 638M5200 638L5199 691M5200 638L5199 691M5200 638L5300 622M5200 638L5200 638"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5200 638h0M5199 691h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0M5200 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Detmold. <a href="/atlas?year=1854&amp;focus=principality-of-lippe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
